The student has a liter of 0.100 M strong acid solution. They want to prepare a buffer. To make a buffer, you typically mix a weak acid with its conjugate base or a weak base with its conjugate acid. 

Since the solution is already a strong acid, it doesn't have any significant conjugate base present. So, to create a buffer, the student needs to add something that will act as the conjugate base. 

If you add a strong acid (option A), that would just make the solution more concentrated with the same acid, which doesn't help in creating a buffer. 

If you add a weak acid (option B), that would just increase the concentration of the acid without providing a significant amount of conjugate base. 

If you add a weak base (option C), that would react with the strong acid, neutralizing it, which would not create a buffer but instead just a solution of the salt. 

Therefore, the best option is to add a strong base (option D). When you add a strong base to the strong acid solution, it will react to form the conjugate base of the acid. For example, if the acid is HCl, adding NaOH will produce Cl^- as the conjugate base. This way, the solution will contain the weak acid (HCl) and its conjugate base (Cl^-), forming a buffer.

So, the correct answer is D.
